  "cfg": {
    "locomotion": {
      "mode": "static",
      "allowedRadius": 0,
      "turnMode": "snap"
    },
    "outline": {
      "version": 1,
      "course": {
        "title": "VSEPR Theory: Predicting Molecular Shape",
        "goal": "Given any molecular formula, draw a valid Lewis structure, count electron domains, and correctly predict electron-domain geometry, molecular geometry, and bond angles.",
        "paceNote": "Learning outline generated from the knowledge graph"
      },
      "progress": {
        "completedSectionIds": []
      },
      "chapters": [
        {
          "id": "ch_msgpywdp9rrx",
          "title": "Foundations: Terms & Lewis Structures",
          "summary": "Establish the vocabulary and the Lewis-structure skill VSEPR depends on.",
          "sections": [
            {
              "id": "sec_msgpywdpwjs9",
              "title": "The Language of VSEPR",
              "type": "reading",
              "summary": "Important Terms section, pg. 1",
              "purpose": "Define electron domain, domain vs molecular geometry, bond angle, Lewis structure, octet rule, formal charge.",
              "vr": {},
              "reading": {
                "chunks": [
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzgndpe23",
                    "title": "Valence electrons: the starting count",
                    "html": "<p><strong>Valence electrons</strong> are the outer-shell electrons an atom uses for bonding. To analyze any molecule, you first <em>sum</em> the valence electrons contributed by every atom.</p><p>For an ion, adjust: add electrons for negative charge, subtract for positive charge.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "CO2 has one carbon (4 valence e-) and two oxygens (6 each). How many total valence electrons?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "16",
                      "explanation": "4 + 6 + 6 = 16 valence electrons."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzgndzjn1",
                    "title": "The octet rule",
                    "html": "<p>The <strong>octet rule</strong>: atoms gain, lose, or share electrons to fill their outer shell with <strong>8 electrons</strong>. When drawing structures, aim to give each atom an octet.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "In your own words, what is the key idea of “The octet rule”?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "Octet rule & exceptions",
                      "explanation": "Name the concept and one distinguishing detail."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzgnd6vks",
                    "title": "Exceptions to the octet",
                    "html": "<p>Not every atom follows the octet:</p><ul><li><strong>Odd electrons</strong> — molecules like NO, NO2, ClO2 can't complete an octet.</li><li><strong>Fewer than 8</strong> — H holds only 2; B and Be often have less.</li><li><strong>Hypervalent</strong> — period-3-and-below central atoms (e.g., PF5, SF4) can exceed 8.</li></ul>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "Which molecule can have MORE than 8 electrons on its central atom?",
                      "options": [
                        "H2O",
                        "SF4",
                        "NH3",
                        "CH4"
                      ],
                      "answer": "SF4",
                      "explanation": "Sulfur is a period-3 atom, so it can be hypervalent and hold more than an octet."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzgndgq9v",
                    "title": "Lewis structure = the 2D map",
                    "html": "<p>A <strong>Lewis structure</strong> is a 2-dimensional drawing showing how a molecule's atoms bond, plus any lone pairs of electrons. It's the map you build before predicting shape.</p><p><strong>Formal charge</strong> helps choose the best structure when several are possible — ideally each atom's formal charge is 0.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "In your own words, what is the key idea of “Lewis structure = the 2D map”?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "Electron-domain geometry",
                      "explanation": "Name the concept and one distinguishing detail."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "A simple Lewis structure of CO2 showing C double-bonded to two O atoms, with lone pairs drawn as dots on the oxygens."
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzgnd9tnn",
                    "title": "Electron domain = one region",
                    "html": "<p>An <strong>electron domain</strong> (also called the <em>steric number</em>) is a region around an atom where electrons are likely found — bonding or nonbonding.</p><p>Water (H2O) has <strong>four domains</strong>: 2 single bonds plus 2 lone pairs.</p>",
                    "followUp": null,
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzgndwlzb",
                    "title": "Count DOMAINS, not atoms or bond lines",
                    "html": "<p>Here's the trap most students fall into: they count each bond <em>line</em> separately, so a double bond looks like two things. It isn't.</p><p><strong>The rule:</strong> a lone pair, a single bond, a double bond, and a triple bond each count as exactly <strong>ONE domain</strong>. A domain is a <em>region</em>, not a line.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "Hydrogen cyanide, HCN, has: 1 single bond (H–C), 1 triple bond (C≡N), and 1 lone pair on N. How many electron domains are around the carbon?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "2",
                      "explanation": "Around C there are two regions: the H–C single bond (1 domain) and the C≡N triple bond (1 domain). The triple bond counts as ONE domain, not three — so carbon has 2 domains."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "Interactive Lewis structure of CO2: each region (the two C=O double bonds) highlighted as a single domain marker, with a running count showing '2 domains'; annotation notes a double bond collapses to one domain."
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzgndo3ko",
                    "title": "Two geometries, two questions",
                    "html": "<p><strong>Electron-domain geometry</strong> = the arrangement of ALL electron domains around the central atom.</p><p><strong>Molecular geometry</strong> = the arrangement of just the <em>atoms</em> (lone pairs are not described, though they still push).</p><p>Same molecule, two different answers — keep them separate.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "Why can a molecule's electron-domain geometry differ from its molecular geometry?",
                      "options": [
                        "Atoms move after bonding",
                        "Molecular geometry ignores lone-pair positions and describes only atoms",
                        "Bond angles are always 180°",
                        "Electron domains don't affect shape"
                      ],
                      "answer": "Molecular geometry ignores lone-pair positions and describes only atoms",
                      "explanation": "Domain geometry counts all domains; molecular geometry names only where the atoms sit, so lone pairs change the name."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "Side-by-side of water: left shows electron-domain geometry (4 domains, tetrahedral) with lone pairs visible; right shows molecular geometry (bent) with only atoms shown."
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzgndoulp",
                    "title": "Bond angle",
                    "html": "<p>A <strong>bond angle</strong> is the angle between two adjacent bonds on the same atom. Bond angles are <em>affected by all domains</em> (including lone pairs), but they only <em>describe</em> the angle between bonding atoms.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "In your own words: what is the ONE rule for turning a bond line count into a domain count?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "Each single, double, or triple bond and each lone pair counts as one domain — count regions, not lines.",
                      "explanation": "This is the key insight: domains are regions of electron density, so multiple bonds and lone pairs each count once."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  }
                ]
              },
              "h5": {
                "prompt": "",
                "html": "",
                "status": "idle",
                "followUp": null
              },
              "quiz": {
                "items": []
