              "buildStatus": "done"
            },
            {
              "id": "sec_msgpywdppde4",
              "title": "Building a Lewis Structure Step by Step",
              "type": "h5",
              "summary": "Drawing a Lewis Structure procedure + CO2 example, pg. 2",
              "purpose": "Interactively walk the 6-step procedure, tagging domains as you go so multiple bonds collapse to one domain.",
              "vr": {},
              "reading": {
                "chunks": []
              },
              "h5": {
                "prompt": "Build a 2D interactive titled 'Domain Counter: Build CO2, Then Count' that installs the aha 'count DOMAINS not atoms'. LAYOUT: left = a step-by-step Lewis-structure builder for CO2; right = a live 'Domain Count' panel with a big number and a legend.\n\nPHASE 1 — GUIDED BUILD (walk the 6-step procedure, covers n1,n2,n3,n4). Show a horizontal step tracker (steps 1-6). Each step reveals one action the student confirms by clicking:\n1) 'Total valence e-': show C=4, O=6, O=6, student clicks to sum -> 16 e-. \n2) 'Central atom': student clicks the least-electronegative atom among {C,O,O}; correct=C (feedback if O clicked: 'O is more electronegative — pick the least').\n3) 'Arrange bonds': auto-place O–C–O with single bonds.\n4) 'Fill outer octets': clicking each O fills lone pairs; running e- used counter shows 16/16.\n5) 'Extra e- on central': none left — button confirms.\n6) 'Central octet check': C has only 4 e- (no octet) -> student clicks 'form double bonds', animate two O lone pairs collapsing into two C=O double bonds. Final structure O=C=O with 2 lone pairs on each O.\n\nPHASE 2 — PREDICT THEN CONSTRUCT THE AHA (n7). Before counting, ask the student to PREDICT via a numeric input: 'How many electron domains around the central C?' Capture their answer. MANY will type 4 (counting each bond line of the two double bonds) — this is the target misconception. Then enter TAGGING MODE: the student clicks each region around C to tag it as ONE domain. When they click a C=O double bond, the TWO bond lines visibly merge/pulse into a SINGLE highlighted domain marker labeled 'double bond = 1 domain'. The right-panel count increments by exactly 1 per tagged region. After tagging both double bonds, the count reads 2 (not 4). If their prediction was >2, show a contrast callout: 'You predicted N. Atoms/bond-lines counted separately = trap. Domains: a double bond is still ONE region -> 2 domains.'\n\nCONTRAST WIDGET: a small toggle to load H2O beside CO2, letting student tag its regions -> 2 single bonds + 2 lone pairs = 4 domains, reinforcing that a lone pair also counts as one domain and that CO2's two doubles are NOT four.\n\nFEEDBACK: every click gives immediate right/wrong coloring; a summary bar states 'Rule: lone pair, single, double, triple bond each = 1 domain.' DATA to use: CO2 (2 domains, both bonding, linear 180°) and H2O (4 domains, 2 bonding + 2 lone) from the source. No external assets; render atoms as circles, bonds as line pairs, domain markers as translucent wedges.",
                "html": "<div style=\"font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;padding:16px\"><h3>Building a Lewis Structure Step by Step</h3>\n<select id=\"h5-pick\"><option>Valence electrons</option><option>Choosing the central atom</option><option>Octet rule & exceptions</option><option>Drawing a Lewis structure</option><option>Counting electron domains</option></select>\n<button id=\"h5-go\" type=\"button\">Show tip</button>\n<pre id=\"h5-out\"></pre>\n<script>\nconst tips=[\"Sum valence electrons for any molecule/ion correctly.\",\"Pick the least electronegative atom as central.\",\"State the octet rule and name exceptions (odd e-, H/B/Be, hypervalent period 3+).\",\"Produce a valid Lewis structure using the 6-step procedure.\",\"Count domains treating each multiple bond and lone pair as one domain.\"];\ndocument.getElementById('h5-go').onclick=()=>{\n  const i=document.getElementById('h5-pick').selectedIndex;\n  document.getElementById('h5-out').textContent=tips[i]||'';\n};\n</script></div>",
                "status": "ready",
                "followUp": {
                  "enabled": true,
                  "type": "short",
                  "question": "C2H2 is H–C≡C–H. How many electron domains are around each carbon, and why?",
                  "options": [
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    ""
                  ],
                  "answer": "2 domains around each carbon.",
                  "explanation": "Each C has one triple bond (to the other C) and one single bond (to H). A triple bond counts as ONE domain, just like a single bond, so 1 + 1 = 2 domains — you count regions, not bond lines. That gives linear geometry, 180°."
                },
                "interactionKind": "predict"
              },
              "quiz": {
