            {
              "id": "sec_msgpywdpnr34",
              "title": "The Full Geometry Table",
              "type": "h5",
              "summary": "3D representation chart, pg. 3",
              "purpose": "Match domain counts and bonding/nonbonding combos to names and bond angles across the whole catalog.",
              "vr": {},
              "reading": {
                "chunks": []
              },
              "h5": {
                "prompt": "Build an interactive 2D 'VSEPR Geometry Builder' that constructs two AHA insights. LAYOUT: left = a central atom (gray circle) with electron domains drawn as colored spokes/balloons radiating out; right = live readout panel showing 'Total domains', 'Bonding', 'Lone pairs', 'Electron-domain geometry', 'Molecular geometry', 'Bond angle'. CONTROLS: (a) a slider or +/- buttons for TOTAL DOMAINS 2→6; (b) a stepper for how many of those domains are LONE PAIRS (0 up to total-1); (c) a 'Predict first' step and a 'Release / Spread out' button. INTERACTION FLOW: When the student sets total domains and BEFORE pressing Release, prompt them to type/pick the predicted bond angle. On Release, animate the domains springing apart to maximum-separation positions (2→180° linear, 3→120° trigonal planar, 4→109.5° tetrahedral, 5→90/120° trigonal bipyramidal, 6→90° octahedral). This builds aha1: repulsion alone sets the arrangement — no memorization. AHA3 CONSTRUCTION: lone-pair domains are drawn as translucent 'balloon' clouds (no atom at the tip), bonding domains have a solid atom ball at the tip. Add a toggle 'Show lone pairs / Atoms only'. With lone pairs ON the arrangement stays at the electron-domain geometry name (e.g. tetrahedral for 4 domains). Switch to 'Atoms only' and the widget hides the lone-pair balloons and re-labels using ONLY the atom positions: 4 domains/1 lone pair reads 'trigonal pyramidal', 4/2 reads 'bent', 2/0 stays linear, etc. The bond-angle readout must visibly DROP below the ideal (e.g. 109.5° → <109.5°, and <<109.5° for two lone pairs) with a note 'lone pairs push harder, shrinking the angle'. Preload quick-scenario buttons matching the source catalog: CO2 (2 dom, 0 LP → linear/linear/180°), H2O (4 dom, 2 LP → tetrahedral/bent/<109.5°), NH3 (4 dom, 1 LP → tetrahedral/trigonal pyramidal/<109.5°), SF4 (5 dom, 1 LP → trigonal bipyramidal/sawhorse), ClF4- (6 dom, 2 LP → octahedral/square planar/90°). FEEDBACK: after each prediction, compare student's guessed angle to actual and show a one-line explanation. The core takeaway text updates: 'Electron-domain geometry counts ALL domains; molecular geometry names only the atoms — lone pairs bend the shape without being seen.' Use figure picture_01 (the 3D geometry chart) styling as reference for the shape labels. Emphasize the contrast: same total domains can give different molecular shapes purely from lone-pair count.",
                "html": "<div style=\"font-family:system-ui,Arial;max-width:900px;margin:auto;padding:12px;background:#0f1420;color:#e8eef7\">\n<h2 style=\"color:#7fd1ff\">VSEPR Geometry Builder</h2>\n<p style=\"font-size:14px;color:#aac\">Set total domains and lone pairs, predict the bond angle, then <b>Release</b> to watch repulsion spread the domains apart.</p>\n\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:16px\">\n  <div style=\"flex:1;min-width:320px;background:#161d2c;border-radius:10px;padding:8px\">\n    <svg id=\"sv\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 320\" style=\"width:100%;background:#0b0f18;border-radius:8px\"></svg>\n    <div style=\"margin-top:8px;text-align:center\">\n      <button onclick=\"release()\" style=\"background:#2a9d5c;color:#fff;border:0;padding:8px 16px;border-radius:6px;cursor:pointer\">Release / Spread out</button>\n      <button onclick=\"toggleLP()\" id=\"lpb\" style=\"background:#4560c0;color:#fff;border:0;padding:8px 12px;border-radius:6px;cursor:pointer\">Show lone pairs</button>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"flex:1;min-width:280px\">\n    <div style=\"background:#161d2c;padding:10px;border-radius:10px\">\n      <label>Total domains: <b id=\"tl\">4</b></label><br>\n      <input type=\"range\" id=\"tot\" min=\"2\" max=\"6\" value=\"4\" style=\"width:100%\" oninput=\"setTot(this.value)\"><br>\n      <label>Lone pairs: <b id=\"ll\">0</b></label><br>\n      <input type=\"range\" id=\"lp\" min=\"0\" max=\"3\" value=\"0\" style=\"width:100%\" oninput=\"setLP(this.value)\">\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background:#161d2c;padding:10px;border-radius:10px;margin-top:10px\">\n      <b>Predict the bond angle first:</b><br>\n      <select id=\"guess\" style=\"width:100%;padding:4px;margin-top:4px\">\n        <option>180°</option><option>120°</option><option>109.5°</option>\n        <option>90°</option><option>&lt;109.5°</option><option>&lt;&lt;109.5°</option>\n      </select>\n      <div id=\"fb\" style=\"margin-top:6px;font-size:13px;min-height:32px;color:#ffd27f\"></div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background:#101623;padding:10px;border-radius:10px;margin-top:10px;font-size:14px\">\n      <div>Total domains: <b id=\"rTot\">4</b></div>\n      <div>Bonding: <b id=\"rB\">4</b></div>\n      <div>Lone pairs: <b id=\"rL\">0</b></div>\n      <div>Electron-domain geometry: <b id=\"rE\" style=\"color:#7fd1ff\">tetrahedral</b></div>\n      <div>Molecular geometry: <b id=\"rM\" style=\"color:#8fffb0\">tetrahedral</b></div>\n      <div>Bond angle: <b id=\"rA\" style=\"color:#ffd27f\">109.5°</b></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<div style=\"margin-top:10px\">\n  <b style=\"font-size:13px\">Quick scenarios:</b>\n  <button onclick=\"preset(2,0)\" style=\"margin:2px\" class=\"p\">CO₂</button>\n  <button onclick=\"preset(4,2)\" class=\"p\">H₂O</button>\n  <button onclick=\"preset(4,1)\" class=\"p\">NH₃</button>\n  <button onclick=\"preset(5,1)\" class=\"p\">SF₄</button>\n  <button onclick=\"preset(6,2)\" class=\"p\">ClF₄⁻</button>\n</div>\n\n<p id=\"take\" style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:14px;background:#12241a;padding:10px;border-radius:8px;color:#bfeecb\">\nElectron-domain geometry counts ALL domains; molecular geometry names only the atoms — lone pairs bend the shape without being seen.</p>\n\n<style>.p{background:#233;color:#cde;border:1px solid #456;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:6px;cursor:pointer;margin:2px}</style>\n\n<script>\nvar svg=document.getElementById('sv'),tot=4,lp=0,showLP=false,released=false;\nvar edg={2:\"linear\",3:\"trigonal planar\",4:\"tetrahedral\",5:\"trigonal bipyramidal\",6:\"octahedral\"};\nvar idealAngle={2:\"180°\",3:\"120°\",4:\"109.5°\",5:\"90/120°\",6:\"90°\"};\n// angle positions (degrees, 0=up) for released state\nfunction positions(n){\n  if(n==2)return[0,180];\n  if(n==3)return[0,120,240];\n  if(n==4)return[0,120,210,300]; // pseudo-tetra layout in 2D\n  if(n==5)return[0,72,144,216,288];\n  return[0,60,120,180,240,300];\n}\nfunction molName(t,l){\n  if(l==0)return edg[t];\n  if(t==2)return \"linear\";\n  if(t==3)return l==1?\"bent\":\"linear\";\n  if(t==4)return l==1?\"trigonal pyramidal\":(l==2?\"bent\":\"linear\");\n  if(t==5)return l==1?\"sawhorse\":(l==2?\"T-shaped\":\"linear\");\n  if(t==6)return l==1?\"square pyramidal\":(l==2?\"square planar\":\"T-shaped\");\n  return edg[t];\n}\nfunction angleReadout(t,l){\n  if(l==0)return idealAngle[t];\n  if(t==2)return \"180°\";\n  if(t==6&&l==2)return \"90°\";\n  if(l==1)return \"<\"+idealAngle[t];\n  return \"<<\"+idealAngle[t];\n}\nfunction draw(){\n  var pos=positions(tot),cx=160,cy=160,R=released?110:38;\n  var s='';\n  s+='<circle cx=\"'+cx+'\" cy=\"'+cy+'\" r=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" stroke=\"#ccc\"/>';\n  s+='<text x=\"'+cx+'\" y=\"'+(cy+5)+'\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#111\" font-size=\"13\">A</text>';\n  // lone pairs occupy first 'lp' positions\n  for(var i=0;i<tot;i++){\n    var isLone=i<lp;\n    var a=(pos[i]-90)*Math.PI/180;\n    var x=cx+R*Math.cos(a),y=cy+R*Math.sin(a);\n    if(isLone && !showLP && released) continue; // hidden in atoms-only\n    var col=isLone?'#c060ff':'#ff8c42';\n    s+='<line x1=\"'+cx+'\" y1=\"'+cy+'\" x2=\"'+x+'\" y2=\"'+y+'\" stroke=\"'+col+'\" stroke-width=\"'+(isLone?9:5)+'\" '+(isLone?'opacity=\"0.4\"':'')+'/>';\n    if(isLone){\n      s+='<ellipse cx=\"'+x+'\" cy=\"'+y+'\" rx=\"18\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#c060ff\" opacity=\"0.28\"/>';\n      s+='<text x=\"'+x+'\" y=\"'+(y+4)+'\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#e6c0ff\" font-size=\"10\">LP</text>';\n    } else {\n      s+='<circle cx=\"'+x+'\" cy=\"'+y+'\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#ffb27f\" stroke=\"#fff\"/>';\n    }\n  }\n  s+='<text x=\"160\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7fd1ff\" font-size=\"13\">'+edg[tot]+'</text>';\n  svg.innerHTML=s;\n}\nfunction update(){\n  document.getElementById('tl').textContent=tot;\n  document.getElementById('ll').textContent=lp;\n  document.getElementById('lp').max=tot-1;\n  document.getElementById('rTot').textContent=tot;\n  document.getElementById('rB').textContent=tot-lp;\n  document.getElementById('rL').textContent=lp;\n  document.getElementById('rE').textContent=edg[tot];\n  // molecular name depends on showLP\n  var mol = (showLP)? edg[tot] : molName(tot,lp);\n  document.getElementById('rM').textContent=mol;\n  document.getElementById('rA').textContent=angleReadout(tot,lp);\n  draw();\n}\nfunction setTot(v){tot=+v;if(lp>tot-1)lp=tot-1;document.getElementById('lp').value=lp;released=false;clearfb();update();}\nfunction setLP(v){lp=+v;released=false;clearfb();update();}\nfunction clearfb(){document.getElementById('fb').textContent='';}\nfunction toggleLP(){showLP=!showLP;document.getElementById('lpb').textContent=showLP?'Atoms only':'Show lone pairs';update();}\nfunction release(){\n  released=true;draw();\n  // feedback comparing guess\n  var g=document.getElementById('guess').value;\n  var actual=angleReadout(tot,lp).replace('&lt;','<');\n  var fb=document.getElementById('fb');\n  var msg;\n  if(g.replace('&lt;','<')===actual){msg=\"✓ Correct! Actual = \"+actual+\".\";}\n  else{msg=\"Actual = \"+actual+\". You guessed \"+g.replace('&lt;','<')+\".\";}\n  if(lp>0)msg+=\" Lone pairs push harder, shrinking the angle below \"+idealAngle[tot]+\".\";\n  else msg+=\" With no lone pairs the ideal \"+idealAngle[tot]+\" holds.\";\n  fb.innerHTML=msg;\n  document.getElementById('take').innerHTML=\n   \"Same total domains (\"+tot+\") give \"+edg[tot]+\" electron-domain geometry, but with \"+lp+\" lone pair(s) the <b>molecular</b> shape is <b>\"+molName(tot,lp)+\"</b>. Toggle 'Atoms only' to hide the lone-pair balloons.\";\n}\nfunction preset(t,l){tot=t;lp=l;document.getElementById('tot').value=t;document.getElementById('lp').max=t-1;document.getElementById('lp').value=l;released=false;showLP=false;document.getElementById('lpb').textContent='Show lone pairs';clearfb();update();}\nupdate();\n</script>\n</div>",
                "status": "ready",
                "followUp": {
                  "enabled": true,
                  "type": "short",
                  "question": "A molecule has 4 electron domains around its central atom, 3 bonding and 1 lone pair. Name its electron-domain geometry, its molecular geometry, and describe its bond angle versus 109.5°.",
                  "options": [
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    ""
                  ],
                  "answer": "Electron-domain geometry: tetrahedral. Molecular geometry: trigonal pyramidal. Bond angle: slightly less than 109.5°.",
                  "explanation": "All 4 domains arrange tetrahedrally (electron-domain geometry counts every domain). But molecular geometry names only the 3 bonded atoms, giving a trigonal pyramid. The lone pair repels harder than bonding pairs, squeezing the bond angle below the ideal 109.5° — exactly the NH3 case from the practice set."
                },
                "interactionKind": "explore"
              },
              "quiz": {
                "items": []
              },
              "covers": [
