              "buildStatus": "done"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ch_msgpywdp9elc",
          "title": "Applying VSEPR",
          "summary": "Work the full prediction pipeline on real molecules and verify mastery.",
          "sections": [
            {
              "id": "sec_msgpywdpkfh9",
              "title": "Worked Example: CO2 End to End",
              "type": "reading",
              "summary": "CO2 VSEPR example, pg. 3",
              "purpose": "Trace the full pipeline from Lewis structure to linear, 180° geometry.",
              "vr": {},
              "reading": {
                "chunks": [
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzxqilw9l",
                    "title": "Step 1: Count Valence Electrons for CO₂",
                    "html": "<p>Start by summing valence electrons. Carbon contributes 4, and each oxygen contributes 6.</p><p>4 + (2 × 6) = <strong>16 valence electrons</strong> total.</p>",
                    "followUp": null,
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzxqi0hx9",
                    "title": "Step 2–3: Central Atom & Skeleton",
                    "html": "<p>Carbon is the least electronegative atom, so it sits in the center. Place the two oxygens on either side, each joined to carbon by a bonding pair: <strong>O–C–O</strong>.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "In your own words, what is the key idea of “Step 2–3: Central Atom & Skeleton”?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "CO2 worked example",
                      "explanation": "Name the concept and one distinguishing detail."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "Simple Lewis skeleton showing O-C-O with single bonds, carbon in the middle"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzxqifztd",
                    "title": "Step 4–6: Complete Octets → Double Bonds",
                    "html": "<p>Fill in electrons on the outer oxygens first. When you check carbon, it lacks a full octet. Convert an outer lone pair from each oxygen into a bond, forming <strong>two double bonds</strong>: O=C=O.</p><p>Now every atom has an octet and all 16 electrons are used.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "After filling outer octets, carbon was short on electrons. What move fixed this?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "Forming double bonds using the outer oxygens' lone pairs.",
                      "explanation": "When the central atom lacks an octet, outer lone pairs become double (or triple) bonds — giving O=C=O."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "Final CO2 Lewis structure O=C=O with two lone pairs on each oxygen"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzxqitvy9",
                    "title": "Step 2 of VSEPR: Count the Domains",
                    "html": "<p>Now count electron domains around carbon. Look at the finished structure O=C=O: there are two <strong>double bonds</strong> and no lone pairs on carbon.</p><p>Here is the trap: a double bond looks like two lines, so it's tempting to count each as two domains. But a domain is a <em>region</em> where electrons live, not a count of bonds or pairs.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "CO₂ has two double bonds on carbon. How many electron domains is that?",
                      "options": [
                        "4, because each double bond is 2 bonds",
                        "2, because each double bond is one region",
                        "3, counting the carbon too",
                        "1, since it's all one molecule"
                      ],
                      "answer": "2, because each double bond is one region",
                      "explanation": "Each double bond = ONE domain. Two double bonds = 2 domains, not 4."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "O=C=O with each double bond circled as ONE shaded region labeled 'domain', showing count = 2"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzxqi6xh0",
                    "title": "The Key: Count Domains, Not Atoms or Bonds",
                    "html": "<p>Here's the rule that unlocks every problem: you count <strong>domains, not atoms and not bond lines</strong>. A lone pair, a single bond, a double bond, and a triple bond each count as exactly <strong>ONE domain</strong>.</p><p>So CO₂'s two double bonds = <strong>2 domains</strong>, both bonding.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "HCN has the structure H–C≡N. How many domains surround the carbon?",
                      "options": [
                        "3",
                        "2",
                        "4",
                        "1"
                      ],
                      "answer": "2",
                      "explanation": "Carbon has one single bond (H–C) = 1 domain, and one triple bond (C≡N) = 1 domain. A triple bond still counts as ONE domain. Total = 2."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgpzxqiwjf5",
                    "title": "Step 3: Read Off the Geometry",
                    "html": "<p>Two bonding domains push apart as far as possible → they point in opposite directions.</p><ul><li><strong>Electron-domain geometry:</strong> linear</li><li><strong>Molecular geometry:</strong> linear (no lone pairs to hide atoms)</li><li><strong>Bond angle:</strong> 180°</li></ul><p>That's the full CO₂ pipeline: Lewis → 2 domains → linear, 180°.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "CS₂ has the structure S=C=S. Predict its molecular geometry and bond angle, and say why.",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "Linear, 180° — carbon has 2 domains (two double bonds), both bonding, so they point 180° apart.",
                      "explanation": "Same pipeline as CO₂: two double bonds = 2 bonding domains = linear at 180°."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "CO2 drawn straight with 180° angle labeled between the two O=C bonds, arrows showing domains pushing apart"
                  }
                ]
              },
              "h5": {
                "prompt": "",
                "html": "",
                "status": "idle",
                "followUp": null
              },
              "quiz": {
                "items": []
              },
