      "ahaKeys": [
        {
          "id": "aha1",
          "insight": "Electron pairs push each other as far apart as possible in 3D — that repulsion alone decides the geometry.",
          "whyKey": "Once you internalize 'domains spread out to maximize distance', you can predict every arrangement without memorizing a table — the table becomes obvious.",
          "misconception": "Students think shape is arbitrary or must be memorized per molecule, not that it follows from one repulsion rule.",
          "buildIdea": "A 3D scene with a central atom and balloon-like domains: the student drags/adds domains and watches them spring apart to the max-separation arrangement (2→linear, 3→trigonal planar, 4→tetrahedral), predicting the angle before releasing.",
          "nodeIds": [
            "n5",
            "n6",
            "n11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "aha2",
          "insight": "You count DOMAINS not atoms: a lone pair, a single, a double, and a triple bond each count as exactly ONE domain.",
          "whyKey": "Every re-skinned problem hinges on getting the domain count right; multiple bonds and lone pairs are the classic traps, and this key defuses both.",
          "misconception": "Students count each bonding pair or each bond line separately, so a double bond looks like two domains.",
          "buildIdea": "An interactive Lewis structure where the student clicks each region to tag it as one domain; a double bond visibly collapses to a single domain marker, and the running count updates.",
          "nodeIds": [
            "n1",
            "n4",
            "n7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "aha3",
          "insight": "Electron-domain geometry counts ALL domains, but molecular geometry describes only the ATOMS — lone pairs bend the shape and shrink the angle without being 'seen'.",
          "whyKey": "This is why H2O is bent not linear and NH3 is pyramidal not tetrahedral; separating the two geometries is what earns full marks on every variant.",
          "misconception": "Students think molecular geometry = electron-domain geometry, so they report tetrahedral for water.",
          "buildIdea": "A 3D water/ammonia scene where the student toggles lone pairs visible/invisible: with them on it's tetrahedral, with them off (atoms only) the remaining shape is bent/pyramidal, and the bond angle readout dips below 109.5°.",
          "nodeIds": [
            "n8",
            "n9",
            "n10",
            "n12"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sourceFilename": "Chem-VSEPR Theory.pdf",
